[h1]Knox Survivors - Survivor NPC Rebuild[/h1]
Knox Survivors adds other human survivors to Project Zomboid while trying to keep the vanilla Zomboid feel. It is inspired by the survivor-world idea behind Superb Survivors, but this rebuild was made from the ground up by .exe.

Survivors use human, player-based NPC bodies and reuse Project Zomboid's existing clothing, items, animations, containers, farming, combat and world systems wherever practical. The goal is a living world - not a free army.

[b]Current release candidate: 0.3.0-rc1[/b]
[b]Built for Project Zomboid 42.20.4. Single-player is the focus. Bugs are expected.[/b]

[h2]Before you play[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]The current rebuild needs the Knox Java runtime loaded when the game starts.[/b] Use either the Steam method or the optional launcher below. Do not use both at the same time.
[*]Use a fresh save for the rebuild and back up saves you care about. Old Knox survivor data is not guaranteed to migrate.
[*]This is a release candidate, not a claim that every edge case is finished.
[/list]

[h2]What is implemented[/h2]
[list]
[*]Persistent human survivors with their own identity, appearance, clothing, inventory, equipment, health, needs, traits, skills and relationships.
[*]World survivors that can roam, scavenge, choose useful equipment, eat, drink, rest, use basic first aid and fight zombies.
[*]Melee combat plus current firearm and reload support. Firearms are newer and may be rougher than melee.
[*]Survivor meetings, dialogue, groups, factions, camps and settlement behavior.
[*]Recruitment and companion orders including Follow, Hold, Return to Base, Dismiss, Go To, Guard and loot-related orders.
[*]Player/faction bases with residents, work areas, storage categories and queued work.
[*]Base jobs including guard, patrol, storage sorting, window barricading, farming, tree cutting, log sawing, corpse hauling/cleanup, trough care and damaged-structure repair.
[*]Companion HUD, survivor information, inventory interaction, activity messages and the current survivor-management UI.
[*]Save/load and off-screen survivor/needs simulation so the living world does not require every NPC to stay physically loaded.
[/list]

[h2]Not being promised as finished[/h2]
Full multiplayer NPC support, autonomous survivor driving, raids/large faction events, away-team resource missions and full construction are not advertised as finished features in this release. Pathing, combat, jobs and save/load can still hit bugs during real Build 42 play.

[h2]Install and play[/h2]
1. Subscribe here and let Steam finish downloading the item.
2. Pick [b]one[/b] launch method below.
3. In Project Zomboid, enable [b]Knox Survivors[/b] for your fresh save.

[b]Method A - Steam-only on Windows (no separate launcher download):[/b]
Open Steam > Project Zomboid > Properties > General > Launch Options and paste this line:

[b]-javaagent:"C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\workshop\content\108600\3749727604\mods\KnoxSurvivors\java\knox-agent.jar"=pz-game --[/b]

Replace the example path with the actual subscribed [b]knox-agent.jar[/b] path and keep the quotes. The Workshop download can be in a different Steam library from the game. Then use Steam's normal Project Zomboid launch mode. This passes the Knox agent directly to Project Zomboid; no terminal window is required. Alternate BAT launch modes need separate setup.

If you previously saved custom game arguments inside the Knox launcher (for example a custom -cachedir), those launcher-only arguments are not automatically transferred to Steam. Add the same arguments after the final [b]--[/b] if you still need them. Normal Project Zomboid saves, sandbox settings, mod settings and memory configuration stay in their normal locations.

Keep existing Steam options: JVM options and other agents go before the single [b]--[/b], game arguments after it. Add Knox only once. Do not combine this with the old cmd/JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS workaround or the Knox launcher. Custom command wrappers need separate validation.

This is a one-time setup, not subscription-only loading. Steam keeps the JAR filename stable through updates. Update the path after moving its library. [b]Before unsubscribing or deleting the JAR, remove only the Knox -javaagent option from Steam[/b]; a missing agent can prevent game startup. Keep the separator if other JVM options need it.

If Windows shows [b]Entry Point Not Found[/b] and names another Java installation's [b]java.dll[/b], use the bundled-runtime isolation setup described in the project README/support instructions. It generates a Steam options line that gives the game's own Java DLLs priority for that process without changing global Windows settings or clearing other agent options. Keep your original options for rollback; a command window may appear in this mode. Compatibility with other mods still needs testing with their actual installed versions.

[b]Method B - optional Knox Survivors Launcher:[/b]
[url=https://github.com/exe-create/KnoxSurvivorsLauncher/releases]Download the latest Knox Survivors Launcher release[/url], extract the complete ZIP for your system and press [b]PLAY KNOX SURVIVORS[/b]. The launcher verifies the Workshop files/checksum and starts the normal game executable. It does not change Project Zomboid's configured memory or set permanent system options.

Steam provides the mod and Knox runtime together. No separate Java installation is required.

[h2]Confirm it loaded[/h2]
After reaching the main menu, open [b]Documents\Zomboid\KnoxIsoPlayer.log[/b] and look for a new timestamped [b]agent start arguments=pz-game[/b] entry from this launch. If the mod says the Java systems are unavailable, check the Steam launch option or remove it before switching to the launcher, then restart Project Zomboid.

